  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT...\n\nEarly this morning, a broken line of showers and storms was \ntraversing southeastward along an advancing cold front across \nportions of southeast Nebraska into northern/northeast Kansas. This \ncluster of storms will generally ride along the axis of greatest \nelevated instability, which is laid out over northeast Kansas, and \nshould remain north and east of a Salina-Chanute line. Meanwhile, \nadditional showers and storms have developed further southwest of \nthe main cluster in a region of mid-level WAA. Despite extensive \nconvective inhibition from warm mid-level temperatures, there's more \nthan enough ascent to overcome this and support continued \nthunderstorm development and maintenance. In general, the intensity \nof storms across the forecast area this morning will somewhat \nlimited given the aforementioned CIN; however, some of the stronger \nconvection could produce 35 to 50 mph winds gusts, brief heavy \nrainfall, and a few lightning strikes. With the leading cold \nfront/outflow boundary exiting southeast Kansas by 12-15Z this \nmorning, rain chances should come to an end along with it.\n\nThrough much of the day the main synoptic front will sag \nsoutheastward across the forecast area. While cooler air will lag \nbehind the initial wind shift allowing for temperatures to rise in \nthe 90s area-wide, drier air will be filtering in throughout the day \nkeeping maximum heat indices in check for most locations. That being \nsaid, portions of far southeast Kansas will remain ahead of the \nfront for much of the day. Compressional warming and moisture \npooling ahead of the frontal boundary in these areas will contribute \nto afternoon temperatures near 100 with dewpoints in the 70s, \nwhich is more than enough to send heat indices above 105. The \ncooler air will finally arrive by tonight sending lows into the \n60s.\n\nThanks to an anomalously strong surface ridge sinking \nsoutheastward across the northern plains and Midwest, a tight \npressure gradient across the area will support breezy northerly \nwinds throughout much of the day on Saturday. This will continue\nthe advection of mild air into the region, especially along and\neast of the Flint Hills where many locations may only see \nafternoon highs in the low 80s. This milder airmass will linger \ninto Saturday night, and lows will dip into the low to mid 60s.\n\n     SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY...\n\nMid range ensemble and deterministic model guidance continues to \nshow a gradual rebound in temperatures, beginning on Sunday, thanks \nin part to gradual height rises aloft from a weak upper ridge \nbuilding across the central plains. While Sunday will feature near, \nor just below average temperatures, seasonably hot conditions will \nreturn as soon as Monday and last through much of next week. \nHowever, the airmass will be much drier next week than it has been \nthis past week. This will generally limit heat indices to the \n90s and low 100s despite air temperatures being about the same. \nIn terms of rain chances next week, there's a number of details \nthat need to be worked out. That being said, some of the model \nguidance is hinting at a weak cold front stalling out across \nNebraska and Kansas which would support the potential for \nthunderstorm development during the afternoon and evening hours \neach day towards the latter half of next week.",
